### Manage the data volumes

`jd volume list` / `show` / `status` report the mounts this project provisions. Volumes are named by
the path you see in the app: `home` for the notebook home directory, `home/<mount_point>` for each
entry of `additional_ebs_mounts` / `additional_efs_mounts`.

`jd volume backup` creates an EBS snapshot and **requires the host to be stopped** — a snapshot of a
running disk is crash-consistent and restores looking fine while missing whatever was still in the
page cache. Each volume keeps one backup; a new one supersedes the previous. `jd down` deletes them.

EFS mounts report `elastic` / `regional` and cannot be backed up: a file system is regional, so it has
no snapshot to restore from.

**Changing `availability_zone` on a live deployment destroys the data on every EBS volume** unless you
back it up first — a block volume cannot cross zones, so terraform must replace it, and a replacement
with nothing to restore from is empty. Nothing refuses the plan. The safe sequence:

```
jd host stop
jd volume backup --all
jd config --restore-volumes --availability-zone <zone>
jd up
```

`--restore-volumes` is the only guard: it refuses if any backup predates the last time the app ran.
Once `ebs_snapshot_ids` is set, do NOT clear or hand-edit it — `snapshot_id` forces replacement, so
"tidying" the map recreates live volumes empty.

### Coding agents (Claude Code, Codex) on Amazon Bedrock

The jupyter image ships `claude` and `codex`, configured to call Amazon Bedrock, and Bedrock access
is on by default. The credentials are those of the `bedrock-app` IAM role, which only the Bedrock
inference policy is attached to: the `bedrock-sidecar` container assumes it from the instance role
every 15 minutes and writes the credentials to `/run/bedrock-creds/credentials.json`, which the
jupyter container mounts read-only and reads through `credential_process`
(`AWS_CONFIG_FILE=/etc/aws/config`). The jupyter container cannot reach the instance metadata service
(IMDS is blocked for its docker network address), so `boto3` and the AWS CLI inside it default to the
`bedrock-app` role, not the instance role: a notebook that relied on the instance role's permissions
no longer has them.

IMPORTANT: every user of the app shares the `bedrock-app` role, and therefore the same Bedrock
credentials. Bedrock usage is not attributable per user: CloudTrail records every call under the
`bedrock-app` role. Make sure the deployer accepts that before leaving Bedrock enabled.

The variables:
- `bedrock_enabled` creates the `bedrock-app` role and the `bedrock-sidecar`; with `false` there is
  no sidecar and no credentials file, so `claude` fails with a credentials error and `codex` with
  `unset: bedrock_enabled is false`
- `bedrock_model_patterns` lists the models the `bedrock-app` role may invoke (IAM wildcards)
- `bedrock_inference_scope` is `geo` (requests stay in the region's geography) or `global`;
  Codex has no geographic profile outside the US, so it needs `global` there
- `claude_code_version` and `codex_version` pin the CLIs in the image; each CLI's default model
  follows its version (Codex's is the one its release recommends for the inference profile prefix,
  set at image build time)

Verify from the jupyter container, headless (no first-run dialogs):

```bash
jd server exec -- "claude -p 'Reply with OK'"
jd server exec -- "codex exec --skip-git-repo-check 'Reply with OK'"
```

IMPORTANT: in an account that never used a model, the first invocation enables it, through an AWS
Marketplace subscription that the `bedrock-app` role cannot start, and accepts the model provider's
EULA. Anthropic models also need a one-time first-time-use form with the deployer's company details.
The deployer must enable each model in Bedrock for the account and region, for example by sending
one prompt from the Bedrock console playground. These are the deployer's decisions: point the user
to TROUBLESHOOT.md, and never invoke a model, accept an agreement or submit the form on their behalf.

`./TROUBLESHOOT.md` covers the failure modes (`OUT_OF_SERVICE` sidecar, missing credentials file,
`AccessDeniedException`, Codex model `unset`) and the AWS CLI commands to enable the models.

